{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' to justify preferring better-corroborated theories without induction, but this fails Popper's own terms because the Tichý/Miller proof demonstrates that verisimilitude cannot be defined or compared within a deterministic framework where all false theories have zero probability content. Since Popper explicitly rejected probabilistic definitions of truth-likeness to avoid induction, and the only remaining non-probabilistic definition (content-based) was proven logically incoherent for comparing false theories, the preference for the 'best-corroborated' theory inevitably smuggles in an inductive assumption: that past severe testing correlates with future success or higher truth-content, which is precisely the principle of uniformity Popper sought to eliminate.","premise":"Popper held that corroboration is not probability and rejected induction; the Tichý/Miller proof shows verisimilitude is undefined for comparing false theories in deterministic frameworks.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot provide a non-inductive basis for theory preference, does Popper's methodology collapse into irrationalism when choosing between equally falsified theories?","target":"27e29ea5ef4c343f9ad3442bbc9f4487b47dea3db92a9fc6abac8596b8caebdf"}]}